#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539120} :activity/id #uuid "ba72ab32-5851-5ec7-a38d-15b4a3c478f7" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048367 :topic/id #uuid "99a15aac-d6e4-5b90-ad24-876fee287562" :topic/math-academy-id 5868} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539038 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204591 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "75e16740-fbdd-57ed-b571-10c98418d67b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538497} {:answer/id #uuid "81877da1-dbe1-5138-92b4-f3362fecddd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538529} {:answer/id #uuid "9dad7b7a-24bc-54dd-bf88-cecd2bf2af0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 \\\\ 13 & -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538608} {:answer/id #uuid "a5f5e832-398a-584d-84e2-7d3d4ee0dd04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -13 & 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538629} {:answer/id #uuid "fbee7505-987c-5333-93f7-661169d5017b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538800}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538608} :answer-field/id #uuid "fbaa73cf-0532-5d23-982d-ace2b400b2ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203461}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "de0e92e4-625e-5a10-9f55-b35504a82613" :question/math-academy-id "q-269813" :question/problem "For $x,y,z\\in {R}^{2},$ consider the following functions: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y(x)=Ax\\,\\text{where}\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & z(y)=[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}{y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & f(z)=Cz\\,\\text{where}\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhat is $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(z(y(x)))$ evaluated at ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]?$" :question/worked-solution "Using the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(z(y(x)))=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}z}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each of the factors in turn.\n\n- The function $f$ acts on a vector $z\\in {R}^{2}$ by multiplying it by $C$ and outputs a vector $f(z)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}z}=C.\n$$\n\n- The function $z$ acts on a vector $y\\in {R}^{2}$ and outputs a vector $z(y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{z}_{1}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{z}_{1}}{∂{y}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{z}_{2}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{z}_{2}}{∂{y}_{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2} & {y}_{1} \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nEvaluating at ${y}_{0}=A{x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- The function $y$ acts on a vector $x\\in {R}^{2}$ by multiplying it by $A$ and outputs a vector $y(x)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=A.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(z(y(x))){|}_{{x}_{0}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}z}{|}_{{z}_{0}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 \\\\ 13 & -5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "1336e42a-6ba6-5fb3-a04f-47a6e4dce174" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539106}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539077 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538947 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45db1775-d75f-5a32-bf4e-5588c6baa812" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538375}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538375} :answer-field/id #uuid "1c4bf129-bdba-5c6e-bbb7-519f5f0734c7" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538814} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ab33217-b058-5d85-ae73-223d6b5d2494" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538599}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538599} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ad3fdf8-4870-5a95-9eb9-08034d7f4484" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538843} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "152e565a-1b64-57b8-9102-58174f36409c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538278}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538278} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec10a1ed-d3eb-56d7-9ce6-53b27fda1bff" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538913}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3c639ae0-88c7-55de-8084-a7f89ff7f5c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-269808" :question/problem "Consider the functions $y(x)=Ax$ and $f(y)=By,$ where $x\\in {R}^{2},y\\in {R}^{3},$ and the matrices $A$ and $B$ are given by\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))$ evaluated at ${x}_{0}=[0,\\,1]^{T}?$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"2\" width=\"30px\">\n${\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))|}_{{x}_{0}}=\\,\\,$\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$11$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "Using the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each of the factors in turn.\n\n- The function $f$ acts on a vector $y\\in {R}^{3}$ by multiplying it by $B$ and outputs a vector $f(y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}=B.\n$$\n\n- The function $y$ acts on a vector $x\\in {R}^{2}$ by multiplying it by $A$ and outputs a vector $y(x)\\in {R}^{3}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=A.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x)) & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =BA \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & 15 \\\\ 11 & 15\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "8ad6dc1a-aade-5e56-ba97-f40b26cefb30" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539038}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539105 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538960 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c87d8c03-c654-5fd1-a126-474ec7651a64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "52" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538700}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538700} :answer-field/id #uuid "191b1726-7502-57e5-8681-508d29bd4ba6" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538813} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c1b1d727-65f5-5ca5-a7ee-9b549c4b6edb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538684}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538684} :answer-field/id #uuid "257b8b19-6314-524b-8139-d750cd288bc1" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538820} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5d05f48b-5b8f-51bc-a0be-e159bb5f3fe4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538434}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538434} :answer-field/id #uuid "471955d2-cf1e-544f-a5b9-3b7a749224a4" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538839} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0da613c8-85a1-597d-9c8a-132534b88588" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538265}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538265} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d9e42c8-0cba-572d-9f30-e6b7d5256db3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538856}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5cda63cf-1401-57bf-8fcd-cd3ff96bbaac" :question/math-academy-id "q-269771" :question/problem "Consider the functions $y:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{2}$ and $f:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2},$ defined by\n\n$$\ny(x)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{1}+{x}_{2}{x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,f(y)=[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}-{y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is \n$\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))$ evaluated at \n${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]?$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"2\" width=\"30px\">\n${\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))|}_{{x}_{0}}=\\,\\,$\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$38$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$34$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "Using the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each of the factors in turn.\n\n- The function $f$ acts on a vector $y\\in {R}^{2}$ and outputs a vector $f(y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus, the total derivative of $f$ with respect to $y$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{y}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{y}_{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{1}}({y}_{1}-{y}_{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{2}}({y}_{1}-{y}_{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{1}}({y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{2}}({y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2{y}_{1} & 2{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at ${y}_{0}=y({x}_{0})=[\\begin{aligned}3\\cdot 2+1 \\\\ 3+2\\cdot 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 14 & 10\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- The function $y$ acts on a vector $x\\in {R}^{3}$ and outputs a vector $y(x)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus, the total derivative of $y$ with respect to $x$ is a $2\\times 3$ matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{y}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{y}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & \\frac{∂{y}_{1}}{∂{x}_{3}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{y}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{y}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & \\frac{∂{y}_{2}}{∂{x}_{3}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}({x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}({x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{3}}({x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}{x}_{3}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}{x}_{3}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{3}}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}{x}_{3})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & {x}_{1} & 1 \\\\ 1 & {x}_{3} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{{x}_{0}}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x)){|}_{{x}_{0}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 14 & 10\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 38 & 52 & 34\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "dc89e4d6-0c97-5036-ace4-2cc37cf12dbf" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539077}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539106 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539029 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8229e779-8e8f-5d38-beed-cefe14383fd0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538530}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538530} :answer-field/id #uuid "27e2998c-fb66-52e1-9a0e-14ecb29db474" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538825} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b8687a41-891a-5b41-bcf8-26c4e1b21954" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538664}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538664} :answer-field/id #uuid "313f45b7-958c-576f-a02c-e52604f20117" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538830} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e307f5d-2cf8-5149-96d9-4060f3630971" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538268}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538268} :answer-field/id #uuid "94b31743-dd24-58bd-b3c9-5a2a72dc5d7a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538879}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "fcdd0ff0-12c7-557b-8715-01260bbcae30" :question/math-academy-id "q-270275" :question/problem "Consider the functions $y:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ and $f:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{3},$ defined by\n\n$$\ny(x)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}^{3} \\\\ -{x}_{1}{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,f(y)=[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}-{y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{2} \\\\ 2{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}^{3}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is \n$\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))$ evaluated at \n${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]?$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"30px\">\n${\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))|}_{{x}_{0}}=\\,\\,$\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">$-3$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">$2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "Using the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each of the factors in turn.\n\n- The function $f$ acts on a vector $y\\in {R}^{2}$ and outputs a vector $f(y)\\in {R}^{3}.$ Thus, the total derivative of $f$ with respect to $y$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{y}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{y}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂{y}_{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{1}}({y}_{1}-{y}_{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{2}}({y}_{1}-{y}_{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{1}}({y}_{2}^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{2}}({y}_{2}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{1}}(2{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}^{3}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{2}}(2{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}^{3})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2{y}_{2} \\\\ 2 & 3{y}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at ${y}_{0}=y({x}_{0})=[\\begin{aligned}0-{1}^{3} \\\\ -1\\cdot 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- The function $y$ acts on a vector $x\\in {R}^{2}$ and outputs a vector $y(x)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus, the total derivative of $y$ with respect to $x$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{y}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{y}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{y}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{y}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}({x}_{2}-{x}_{1}^{3}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}({x}_{2}-{x}_{1}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}(-{x}_{1}{x}_{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}(-{x}_{1}{x}_{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3{x}_{1}^{2} & 1 \\\\ -{x}_{2} & -{x}_{1}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{{x}_{0}}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x)){|}_{{x}_{0}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "dd4c11e8-e668-5046-aa77-606d9f41c236"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539120 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204577 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2788ba22-a227-53f4-90dd-d5c352334ed1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}28 & 18 \\\\ 44 & 24\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538313} {:answer/id #uuid "4197df66-afcb-5d6f-962d-7c1c812afe7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}28 & 44 \\\\ 18 & 24\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538366} {:answer/id #uuid "42677020-e248-53b0-b94f-e0ebc6c2437f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}18 \\\\ 24\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538368} {:answer/id #uuid "5dfb5629-98bc-54e5-b45f-e48d0667cc5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}8 & 18 \\\\ 44 & 24\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538437} {:answer/id #uuid "e95f7afe-3c38-53ed-8952-94c6506a6fd0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}18 & 24\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538762}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538366} :answer-field/id #uuid "57e030fa-9dbc-5846-9dc8-518d5f807bf1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203395}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "77592e10-440c-570e-8bf1-80fb6d00a4df" :question/math-academy-id "q-268192" :question/problem "For $x,y,z\\in {R}^{2},$ consider the following functions: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y(x)=Ax\\,\\text{where}\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & z(y)=[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & f(z)=Cz\\,\\text{where}\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhat is $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(z(y(x)))$ evaluated at ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]?$" :question/worked-solution "Using the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(z(y(x)))=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}z}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each of the factors in turn.\n\n- The function $f$ acts on a vector $z\\in {R}^{2}$ by multiplying it by $C$ and outputs a vector $f(z)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}z}=C.\n$$\n\n- The function $z$ acts on a vector $y\\in {R}^{2}$ and outputs a vector $z(y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{z}_{1}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{z}_{1}}{∂{y}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{z}_{2}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{z}_{2}}{∂{y}_{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2{y}_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nEvaluating at ${y}_{0}=A{x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}=[\\begin{aligned}2(1) & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2(3)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- The function $y$ acts on a vector $x\\in {R}^{2}$ by multiplying it by $A$ and outputs a vector $y(x)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=A.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(z(y(x))){|}_{{x}_{0}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}z}{|}_{{z}_{0}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}10 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}28 & 44 \\\\ 18 & 24\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "f6ca4762-0e9d-50ee-91d0-ecc6cd70edcf" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539105}}] :activity/title "The Chain Rule for Total Derivatives" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538227}]}
